content.text:
[
  {
    "file": "/repo/proxy.ts",
    "line": 41,
    "summary": "The `x-nonce` request header is set and forwarded but never read anywhere in the app (grep: only proxy.ts sets it; app/layout.tsx explicitly declines to read it), and Next.js itself never reads `x-nonce` — per the official CSP guide, Next extracts the nonce by parsing the `Content-Security-Policy` header present on the *request*. As written, the entire `new Headers(request.headers)` / `requestHeaders.set('x-nonce', ...)` / `NextResponse.next({ request: { headers } })` forwarding machinery carries only a dead header, while the one header that would actually make Next tag its scripts (`Content-Security-Policy` on the forwarded request headers) is missing.",
    "failure_scenario": "Dead code plus a functional gap hiding behind it: because the CSP is set only on the response and not on the forwarded request headers, Next.js never sees the nonce during SSR and does not attach it to its framework/bootstrap scripts — under `script-src 'nonce-...' 'strict-dynamic'` with no 'unsafe-inline' fallback, the app's own scripts are blocked and hydration fails. Either forward the CSP header on the request (making the forwarding real, per the Next.js docs example) or, if the x-nonce read is never added, delete the request-header forwarding entirely and use plain `NextResponse.next()`."
  },
  {
    "file": "/repo/app/layout.tsx",
    "line": 27,
    "summary": "The comment 'Next.js automatically tags its own bootstrap <script> elements with the nonce from the response's CSP header' is factually wrong on two counts: Next.js parses the nonce from the *request*'s Content-Security-Policy header (the one middleware must forward via NextResponse.next({request:{headers}})), not the response header; and since proxy.ts only sets the response header, the described automatic tagging does not happen at all in this codebase.",
    "failure_scenario": "The comment asserts the mechanism is wired up when it is not, so a maintainer debugging 'Refused to execute inline script' CSP violations will read this comment, conclude the nonce plumbing is correct by design, and look elsewhere. It also documents the wrong header source, so a future fix attempt guided by the comment (tweaking the response header) cannot succeed."
  },
  {
    "file": "/repo/proxy.ts",
    "line": 39,
    "summary": "The comment 'Forward the nonce to server components via a request header so layouts can read it via headers() and pass it to <Script> tags they render' describes a consumer that does not exist and directly contradicts app/layout.tsx, whose comment says 'we don't need to read x-nonce here ourselves'. No layout or component reads x-nonce or renders a nonce-bearing <Script>.",
    "failure_scenario": "The two comments give opposite accounts of the same header, so a reader cannot tell whether x-nonce is load-bearing or vestigial. Someone 'cleaning up' per the layout comment deletes the forwarding that a future third-party <Script> would need, or conversely someone preserves dead plumbing forever because proxy.ts claims layouts depend on it. Either state the real (currently: none) consumer or remove the header."
  },
  {
    "file": "/repo/app/layout.tsx",
    "line": 31,
    "summary": "`await headers()` with the result discarded is a side-effect trick to force dynamic rendering. The idiomatic form Next.js documents for exactly this nonce use case is `await connection()` from 'next/server' ('wait for an incoming request to render this page' — shown in the CSP guide's 'Forcing dynamic rendering' section); `export const dynamic = 'force-dynamic'` is the declarative alternative.",
    "failure_scenario": "A discarded `headers()` call looks like leftover debris — a refactor or lint pass ('unused await result') removes it, silently re-enabling static rendering, at which point every request serves a build-time page whose scripts carry no nonce while the proxy stamps a fresh nonce into the CSP header, blocking all JS. `await connection()` states the intent in the API name and cannot be mistaken for dead code. (If the x-nonce read is ever wired up per the docs, `headers()` becomes genuinely used and this resolves itself.)"
  },
  {
    "file": "/repo/proxy.ts",
    "line": 35,
    "summary": "The comment justifies API availability against 'the Edge runtime that Next proxy runs in', but in Next.js 16 (this repo is on 16.2.4) Proxy defaults to the Node.js runtime — the proxy.js docs state 'Proxy defaults to using the Node.js runtime' and the v16.0.0 changelog entry confirms it. The code works either way (Buffer and crypto.randomUUID exist in both), but the stated premise is wrong.",
    "failure_scenario": "Low-stakes but misleading: a maintainer trusting this comment will assume Edge-runtime constraints apply to proxy.ts (no Node APIs, no fs, etc.) and contort future changes around limitations that no longer exist, or waste time reconciling the comment with runtime behavior. Drop the runtime claim or say 'Node.js runtime (Next 16 default for proxy)'."
  }
]

