content.text:
[
  {
    "file": "proxy.ts",
    "line": 42,
    "summary": "Nonce is never delivered to Next.js: the proxy forwards only `x-nonce` on the request but not the `Content-Security-Policy` header. Next.js applies nonces to its own bootstrap/framework <script> tags by parsing the CSP from the *request* headers (getScriptNonceFromHeader), not from the response header as the new comment in app/layout.tsx:27-30 claims. The official Next CSP recipe includes `requestHeaders.set('Content-Security-Policy', csp)` before `NextResponse.next(...)`; that line was omitted.",
    "failure_scenario": "User loads any page in a browser enforcing CSP: Next emits its inline bootstrap script and parser-inserted /_next/static chunk <script> tags without nonces. Under `script-src 'self' 'nonce-X' 'strict-dynamic'`, 'strict-dynamic' makes browsers ignore 'self' for parser-inserted scripts, so every framework script is blocked. The SSR'd HTML paints but nothing hydrates — no button, panel navigation, file upload, or localStorage persistence works; console fills with CSP violations. The invariant 'Next-emitted scripts carry the request nonce' is established nowhere."
  },
  {
    "file": "proxy.ts",
    "line": 58,
    "summary": "The `missing` matcher conditions mean any request carrying `next-router-prefetch` or `purpose: prefetch` bypasses the proxy entirely, so that HTML/RSC response is served with NO Content-Security-Policy header at all. The invariant the diff exists to establish — 'every rendered document carries a nonce CSP' — is not re-established for prefetched documents.",
    "failure_scenario": "Chrome and Safari send `Purpose: prefetch` for <link rel=\"prefetch\">/speculative prefetches of full documents. If a browser prefetches `/` (header present → proxy skipped → response has no CSP, and the layout still renders since `await headers()` doesn't depend on the proxy) and then commits a navigation from that prefetched response, the document runs with zero CSP — no script-src, no frame-ancestors — silently losing the injected-script protection exactly on those navigations. Attacker-controlled fetches can also set these headers to retrieve CSP-less HTML."
  },
  {
    "file": "proxy.ts",
    "line": 22,
    "summary": "No `worker-src` (or `child-src`) directive, so pdfjs-dist's Web Worker (`GlobalWorkerOptions.workerSrc = new URL('pdfjs-dist/build/pdf.worker.min.mjs', import.meta.url)` in app/lib/utils/fileExtraction.ts:26 and app/lib/utils/pdfPropositionParser.ts:443) falls back to `script-src 'self' 'nonce-X' 'strict-dynamic'`, where 'strict-dynamic' causes 'self' to be ignored. Browser handling of `new Worker(url)` under 'strict-dynamic' is inconsistent (only later CSP3 clarifications allow it; Safari/Firefox versions block it).",
    "failure_scenario": "User uploads a .pdf via FileUpload in a browser that doesn't extend 'strict-dynamic' trust to worker creation: the worker script load is refused by CSP. pdfjs either throws (PDF extraction and PDF proposition decomposition fail outright) or degrades to the main-thread 'fake worker' fallback, freezing the UI on large PDFs. Previously (no CSP) the worker always loaded. Fix would be adding `worker-src 'self' blob:`."
  },
  {
    "file": "app/layout.tsx",
    "line": 31,
    "summary": "`await headers()` in the root layout opts the entire app out of static rendering. Before the diff, the single route `/` (a \"use client\" page under a sync layout) was prerendered at build time (○ Static) and served from the full route cache; now every request — including /_not-found — is rendered on-demand (ƒ Dynamic). next.config.ts is empty and no route segment config (`export const dynamic`/`revalidate`) re-establishes caching anywhere, and no ISR/`revalidate` mitigates it.",
    "failure_scenario": "Every page view (and every 404 for a random path) now executes a server render: higher TTFB, per-request server/edge compute, no CDN cacheability of the HTML. Under load or on a small deployment this is a measurable performance regression versus the previous static-file serve. It is also broader than needed: the nonce is only meaningful when the CSP request-header plumbing (see proxy.ts:42 finding) actually works — currently the app pays the dynamic-rendering cost while getting no working nonce in return."
  },
  {
    "file": "proxy.ts",
    "line": 22,
    "summary": "The CSP is applied unconditionally in development too — `script-src` has no 'unsafe-eval' and the matcher has no NODE_ENV carve-out. Next.js dev mode (webpack eval-source-map, React Refresh, dev overlay; Turbopack dev to a lesser extent) relies on eval/inline scripts that a nonce-only strict CSP blocks, which is why the official Next CSP guidance skips or loosens CSP when NODE_ENV === 'development'.",
    "failure_scenario": "Developer runs `npm run dev` and opens localhost:3000: HMR/React Refresh and eval'd source-mapped chunks are blocked with 'Refused to evaluate a string as JavaScript' CSP errors; the dev overlay and hot reloading break, making the dev workflow (documented in CLAUDE.md as the primary command) unusable until the proxy special-cases development. Half-believed for Turbopack (Next 16 default dev bundler) which uses eval less, but the dev overlay's inline bootstrapping is still un-nonced given the request-header omission."
  },
  {
    "file": "proxy.ts",
    "line": 27,
    "summary": "`frame-ancestors 'none'` silently removes a pre-existing capability: before the diff the app could be embedded in an iframe anywhere (no X-Frame-Options or CSP existed). This is a behavior removal beyond the diff's stated goal (script-injection hardening) and is not flagged in the extensive proxy comments, which only document the style-src carve-out.",
    "failure_scenario": "Any existing embedding of the copilot — e.g., a demo iframe on the Live Conversational Threads research page or an internal dashboard embedding localhost/deployed app — breaks after deploy: the browser refuses to render the frame ('refused to display ... frame-ancestors none'). If embedding is desired, the invariant must be re-established with `frame-ancestors 'self' <allowed-origin>`; nothing in the repo records that embedding was considered."
  }
]

